  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 310 AM EDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nEarly this morning, ~990mb low pressure is moving towards Hudson \nBay, with a trailing cold front extending into the Great Lakes and \nalready reaching the western UP. Water vapor imagery reveals drier \nmidlevel air and clearing skies over most of the area, with the \nexception of the south-central and eastern UP. There, SSW flow keeps \nus connected to a more moisture-rich airmass, and is even advecting \nin mid and upper level cloud cover from convection over the Central \nPlains. Some spotty shower activity is also evident over Lake \nMichigan, though cloud cover and convection should dwindle closer to \ndaybreak as the cold front makes its way eastward. Of greater \nconcern will be wind speeds, as steepening lapse rates and deeper \nmixing allow us to tap into stronger wind speeds aloft through the \ndaytime hours. The latest RAP analysis shows a core of 40-50kt flow \nat around the 800-700mb level over MN and pointing towards Lake \nSuperior and the UP. Given the lackluster temperature difference \nbetween the land (or water) surface temperature and the 850 mb \nlevel, mixing is likely to be less than fully efficient. That said, \nthe NBM still shows 60-80% chance of 10m gusts greater than 45 mph \nin the Keweenaw, and 40-60% chance of gusts greater than 40 mph \nelsewhere near the Superior shoreline behind the cold front. Still, \nwill hold off on a Wind Advisory over the Keweenaw given uncertainty \nin the frequency of those higher-end wind gusts. \n\nOtherwise, expect temperatures to warm into the lower to mid 70s for \nmost of the UP, and the upper 70s to lower 80s ahead of the cold \nfront in the eastern UP. Temperatures fall back into the 40s to \nlower 50s under mostly clear skies tonight. \n\nElevated westerlies linger in the eastern UP into Thursday morning \nas the Canadian low departs toward the Canadian Maritimes and \nweakens; then ridging builds into the Upper Midwest for a benign \nweather day. High temperatures should be nearly exactly seasonable, \nwith highs close to 70 and departures of only a few degrees. \n\nThis weekend sees another bout of unsettled weather as a second \nstout low tracks eastward across the Canadian Prairies and towards \nHudson Bay, eventually dragging a cold front through the Great \nLakes. Some of the guidance does feature a secondary low developing \nalong the boundary over the Dakotas and heading towards the UP by \nearly Saturday. Both systems (though not entirely unattached on one \nanother) send tight pressure gradients across the CWA, hinting at \nthe potential for higher winds at times along with precipitation \nchances from late Friday through Saturday. Much of the guidance \nfavors a brief period of ridging to finish out the weekend before \ndeepening low pressure ejects out of the Plains early next week, \nbringing in another period of active weather and blustery winds.",
